  "finding_id": "FND-C9-90da5532",
  "harness_defect": false,
  "harness_review": "§6.2 review: every reviewed completion is the LISTEN that opened the listener's session and the NOTIFY transaction that was missed, both ok (row 1: explicit success; a NOTIFY's COMMIT acknowledged). A misclassification could create this anomaly only if a missed NOTIFY were not committed (it is ok, so committed) or the listener's session were not live (C8 requires LISTEN ok before the NOTIFY and a later ok wait past its 10 s window; the session never broke). The waits were contiguous 1 s waits on the same connection (W6, ADR-0030), and the listener received notifications on that connection before the fault, so the client was able to receive them. Every fault of the run was confirmed and healed (nemesis events), the run is valid (seal, checks.json). Not a HARNESS_DEFECT.",
  "known_status_reasoning": "S19 documents the loss of notifications sent during reconnect windows (a KNOWN_GAP for those, measured as notify-window-loss and not reported); it states that the stream is re-established automatically, which the runs and the source contradict. No alpha-known-gap issue named in RESEARCH S50 matches; nothing was looked up online (AGENTS.md §6). Known status NEW.",
  "minimized_run": "20261014T012149Z-C9-3-real-52de89e0",
  "oracle": "not-applicable (no C3 anomaly)",
  "origin_reasoning": "The behaviour follows from Multigres' gateway code (notification_adapter.go at e3cbdbb); PostgreSQL's own LISTEN/NOTIFY delivers on the listening connection, which is never broken here. Origin MULTIGRES.",
  "run_id": "20261013T230909Z-C9-3-real-bb8a276f",
